::ENG4_U03_L04_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage: Schools should extend lunch periods by ten minutes because students often rush through meals and some throw away food before finishing. What is the claim? {
~A. Students should never eat lunch.#This contradicts the passage.
=B. Schools should extend lunch periods by ten minutes.#Correct. This is the position being argued.
~C. Food waste has no connection to time.#This contradicts the reasoning.
~D. Schools should remove all schedules.#This is not stated.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage: A survey at one school found that 61 percent of respondents wanted a longer lunch period. What is the best evaluation of this evidence? {
=A. It is relevant to student preference but does not by itself prove academic or health benefits.#Correct
~B. It proves every student needs a longer lunch.#This is too broad.
~C. It is unrelated to the argument.#It relates to lunch length.
~D. It proves lunch should be eliminated.#This contradicts the argument.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: Students often rush through meals, and some throw away food because they do not have enough time to eat. How does this support the central idea? {
~A. It proves food has no value.#This is unsupported.
~B. It argues against lunch periods.#This contradicts the claim.
=C. It explains the problem the proposed longer lunch is meant to address.#Correct
~D. It shows students never eat.#This is too absolute.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: The author admits that the argument would be stronger with evidence about nutrition, schedule effects, or focus. What is the author's purpose for including this limitation? {
~A. To prove the entire claim is false#The author does not abandon the claim.
~B. To change the topic to unrelated activities#The limitation is related.
~C. To entertain readers with fiction#This is not fiction.
=D. To show awareness of what evidence is still needed#Correct
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: The argument for longer lunches uses student preference and rushed meals as evidence. Which presentation sentence best evaluates validity? {
~A. The argument is perfect because one survey exists.#This overstates the evidence.
=B. The argument has some valid reasoning because time pressure supports the need for more time, but more evidence is needed to prove broader benefits.#Correct
~C. The argument is invalid because lunch is a word.#This is illogical.
~D. The argument has no claim.#It has a clear claim.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage: A city should add protected bike lanes downtown because protected lanes reduce cyclist injuries and encourage more residents to commute without cars. What is the claim? {
=A. The city should add protected bike lanes downtown.#Correct
~B. Cars should be removed from every road immediately.#This is not stated.
~C. Cyclists should avoid downtown.#This contradicts the passage.
~D. Bike lanes have no purpose.#This contradicts the argument.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage: Protected lanes reduce cyclist injuries and encourage more residents to commute without cars. Which evidence would best strengthen the argument? {
~A. A list of bicycle colors#This is unrelated.
~B. A paragraph about unrelated sports#This is irrelevant.
~C. A statement that streets exist#This is too general.
=D. Local crash data before and after protected lanes were added#Correct
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: Protected lanes reduce cyclist injuries. How does this support the central idea? {
~A. It proves cyclists never get hurt.#This is too absolute.
~B. It shows bike lanes are decorative only.#This contradicts the passage.
=C. It gives a safety reason for adding the lanes.#Correct
~D. It removes the author's purpose.#It supports the purpose.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: The author emphasizes reduced injuries before discussing commuting. What purpose does this choice serve? {
~A. It hides the claim.#The claim is clear.
=B. It foregrounds safety as a strong reason to support the policy.#Correct. The order emphasizes the safety benefit.
~C. It proves commuting is impossible.#This contradicts the passage.
~D. It makes the text fictional.#The passage is informational argument.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: A speaker explains: "Protected bike lanes should be added because they can reduce injuries and support car-free commuting." What makes the explanation clear? {
=A. It states the claim and gives two reasons connected to the claim.#Correct
~B. It avoids the claim.#It states the claim.
~C. It uses unrelated examples.#The reasons are related.
~D. It refuses to explain reasoning.#It gives reasoning.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage: Schools should require financial literacy because students will eventually make decisions about credit, savings, taxes, and loans. What is the reasoning? {
~A. Students should never learn about money.#This contradicts the passage.
~B. Taxes are unrelated to life.#This is false and unsupported.
~C. Financial literacy means avoiding all decisions.#This contradicts the passage.
=D. Students need financial knowledge because they will face financial decisions.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage: Schools should require financial literacy because students will eventually make decisions about credit, savings, taxes, and loans. What additional evidence would strengthen the claim? {
~A. A list of school mascots#This is unrelated.
~B. A recipe for lunch#This is irrelevant.
=C. Data showing graduates often struggle with financial decisions without instruction#Correct
~D. A poem about weather#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: Students will eventually make decisions about credit, savings, taxes, and loans. How does this support the central idea? {
~A. It proves students already know everything.#This contradicts the need for instruction.
=B. It shows financial literacy has practical future relevance.#Correct. The support connects course content to real decisions.
~C. It avoids the topic of finance.#It is directly about finance.
~D. It proves taxes are optional.#This is unsupported.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: The author lists credit, savings, taxes, and loans. Why is this author choice effective? {
=A. It gives concrete examples of financial decisions students may face.#Correct
~B. It distracts from the topic.#The list supports the topic.
~C. It proves students should avoid adulthood.#This is unsupported.
~D. It removes the claim.#The claim remains clear.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: Schools should require financial literacy because students will eventually make decisions about credit, savings, taxes, and loans. Which oral explanation is strongest? {
~A. The argument is logical because all lists are logical.#This is too broad.
~B. The argument has no evidence.#It includes examples.
~C. The argument is about science labs.#This is unrelated.
=D. The argument is logical because the evidence names real financial decisions that the required course could help students prepare for.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage: A city should keep public pools open later in summer because heat waves are lasting longer and not all residents have safe places to cool down. What is the claim? {
~A. Heat waves are impossible.#This contradicts the passage.
~B. Residents never need cooling spaces.#This contradicts the passage.
=C. The city should keep public pools open later in summer.#Correct
~D. Public pools should close permanently.#This contradicts the claim.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage: Heat waves are lasting longer and not all residents have safe places to cool down. What additional evidence would improve validity? {
~A. A list of pool paint colors#This is unrelated.
=B. Local temperature data and usage data from public cooling sites#Correct
~C. A story about winter sports#This is irrelevant.
~D. A statement that summer exists#This is too general.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: Not all residents have safe places to cool down. How does this support the central idea? {
=A. It explains why extended public pool access could matter for community safety.#Correct
~B. It proves all residents have private pools.#This contradicts the passage.
~C. It argues against cooling spaces.#This contradicts the claim.
~D. It makes the argument unrelated to heat.#It is directly related.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: The author uses the phrase "safe places to cool down." What purpose does this word choice serve? {
~A. It makes the argument about sports only.#The wording is about safety.
~B. It removes urgency.#The phrase adds urgency.
~C. It hides the claim.#The claim is clear.
=D. It frames the issue as public safety, not just recreation.#Correct. The wording strengthens the policy purpose.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: The pool argument connects heat waves to access to cooling spaces. Which presentation sentence is strongest? {
~A. The argument is valid because pools are blue.#This is irrelevant.
~B. The argument is invalid because summer exists.#This is illogical.
=C. The argument is valid if evidence shows extended pool hours would give residents safer cooling options during longer heat waves.#Correct
~D. The argument has no claim.#It has a clear claim.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage: A school should keep its library open before classes because students need access to computers, printers, and quiet study space before the day begins. What is the claim? {
~A. Students should avoid computers.#This contradicts the passage.
=B. The school should keep its library open before classes.#Correct
~C. Printers should replace libraries.#This is not stated.
~D. Quiet study has no value.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage: Students need access to computers, printers, and quiet study space before the day begins. Which related argument might challenge this claim? {
=A. Opening the library early may require staffing or security resources the school does not have.#Correct
~B. Computers are made of plastic.#This does not challenge the claim meaningfully.
~C. Libraries contain shelves.#This is not a related counterclaim.
~D. Students have backpacks.#This is irrelevant.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: Students need access to computers, printers, and quiet study space. How does this support the central idea? {
~A. It proves libraries are unnecessary.#This contradicts the passage.
~B. It avoids student needs.#It names student needs.
~C. It proves all students own printers.#This contradicts the need.
=D. It identifies practical needs that early library access could address.#Correct. The support explains the purpose of the policy.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: The author lists computers, printers, and quiet study space. Why is this choice effective? {
~A. It changes the topic to lunch.#This is unrelated.
~B. It removes the claim.#The claim remains.
=C. It makes the benefit of early access specific rather than vague.#Correct
~D. It proves the library should close.#This contradicts the claim.
}
::ENG4_U03_L04_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: A school should keep its library open before classes because students need access to computers, printers, and quiet study space. Which explanation best evaluates reasoning? {
~A. The reasoning is valid because every school building has doors.#This is irrelevant.
=B. The reasoning is valid if the school can show that students lack other reliable access to those resources before class.#Correct
~C. The reasoning is invalid because printers exist.#This is illogical.
~D. The reasoning has no evidence.#It includes examples of needs.
}